</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the concentration of the compound in the original drinking water sample, we need to use the mass balance balance and the given information. The mass balance equation is:

\[
\text{Mass in Original Solution} = \text{Mass in New Solution}
\]

The mass of the compound remains unchanged during the reaction. Therefore, the mass of the compound in the original solution equals the mass of the compound in the new solution.

Let:
- \( L_1 = 100 \) L (weight of the original solution)
- \( C_1 = 0.0125 \) M (concentration of the compound in the new solution)
- \( L_2 = 100 \) mL = 0.10 L (weight of the source solution)
- \( C_2 = 0.0125 \) M (concentration of the compound in the new solution)

The mass balance equation for mass of the compound is:
\[
C_1 \times L_2 = C_2 \times L_1
\]

Substitute the known values:
\[
\text{Weight of the original solution} \times \text{Concentity of the original solution} = \text{Weight of the new solution} \times \text{Concentiction of the new solution}
\]

\[
0.10 \text{ L} \times C_1 = 100 \text{ L} \times 0.0125 \text{ M}
\]

Solve for \( C_1 \):
\[
C_1 = \frac{100 \text{ L} \times 0.0125 \text{ M}}{0.10 \text{ L}}
\]
\[
C_1 = 125 \text{ M}
\]

Thus, the concentration of the compound in the original drinking water sample is \(\boxed{125 \text{ M}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

