To calculate the standard hydrostatic excess of the reaction, we use the formula:

\[
\text{SOH} = \sum \text{M} \times \text{M} \text{ of hydroxide} - \sum \text{M} \times \text{O} \text{ of reactants}
\]

Substituting the given hydrostatic energyes of formation:

\[
\text{SOH} = \text{H}_{2O} \text{of} \text{Ca(OH)2} - (\text{H}_{20} \text{of} \text{KOH} + \text{H}_{20} \text{of} \text{O})
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
\text{SOH} = -986.2 \text{g/mol} - ((-635.1 \text{g/mol}) + (-285.8 \text{g/mol}))
\]

\[
\text{SOH} = -986.2 \text{g/mol} + 635.1 \text{g/mol} + 285.8 \text{g/m}
\]

\[
\text{SOH} = -185.3 \text{g/mol}
\]

The standard enthalpy change for the reaction is \(\boxed{-185.3 \text{ kg/mol}}\).
