\[
p = \frac{G}{p_0} = \frac{M_0 \rho_0 \Delta \rho}{p_0} = \frac{M_0 \rho_0 \Delta \rho}{M_0 \rho_0} = \delta \rho
\]
where \( G \) is the equilibrium pressure, \( p_0 \) is the original pressure, \( M_0 \) is the molar density, \( \rho_0 \) is the original molar rate, and \( \Delta \rho \) is the molar reaction density.

The electroicity pressure is directly proportional to the excess energy \( \delta \rho \).

The electropholarity \( \Phi \) is the energy of the injected reaction molecule per mole of reacting matter. Thus, the equation is:
\[
\Phi = \frac{E}{M_0 \rho_0}
\]
The excess energy is:
\[
\delta \rho = \frac{G}{p_0} - 1
\]
The excess pressure is:
\[
\delta p = \frac{G}{p_0} - 1
\]
The excess pressure is proportional to the excess energy.

The electrophacity \( \Phi \) is the excess energy per mole of reacting matter. Thus, the equation is:
\[
\Delta \phi = \frac{E}{M_0 \rho_0}
\]

The electrophacity is the measure of the excess energy of the injected reaction material per mole of reacting matter.

The excess pressure is the difference between the excess energy and the original energy. Thus, the equation is:
\[
\delta p = \delta \rho - 1
