","The equation of the tangent line to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by:  
\[
f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)
\]  
This equation is derived by the geometric interpretation of the slope of the tangent line and the point-slope form of a line.  

The slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \) is the derivative of \( f \) at \( x = a \), which is \( f'(a) \).  

The equation of a line passing through the point \( (a, f(a)) \) with slope \( f'(a) \) is:  
\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]  
Rearranging this equation gives:  
\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
\]  
This is the equation of the tangent line.  

Final Answer:  
\[
\boxed{y = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)}
\]",relevance,"# Task Description
