","To find out how far from the start when b catches up to a, we first need to determine the distance a has been away from the start when b starts cycling.

A's speed = 10 kmph
B's speed = 20 kmph

A starts in the start and b starts 3 hours after a starts.

After 3 hours, A's distance from the start = Speed of A * Time taken
Distance = 10 kmph * 3 hours
Distance = 30 km

Now, b starts cycling at 20 kmph. To find out how long it will take for b to catch up to a, we need to calculate the relative speed between b and a.

Relative speed = Speed of B - Speed of A
Relative speed = 20 kmph - 10 kmph
Relative speed = 10 kmph

Now, we can calculate the time it will take for b to cover the 30 km gap between b and a.

Time to catch up = Distance between b and a / Relative speed
Time to catch up = 30 km / 10 kmph
Time to catch up = 3 hours

So, b will catch up to a after 3 hours after b starts cycling.

Now, we can calculate the distance from the start when b catches up to a.

Distance covered by b in 3 hours = Speed of B * Time taken
Distance covered by b = 20 kmph * 3 hours
Distance covered by b = 60 km

Therefore, b catches up to a after 60 km from the start.

So, the final answer is $\boxed{60}$ .",relevance,"# Task Description
