The time delay due to the ship's speed is due to the translational motion of the ship. This is given by the formula for time deceleration:

\[
t = \frac{t_0}{\sqrt{1 - v^2/v_0^2}}
\]

where:
- \( t \) is the time elapsed on the object (in this case, the car)
- \( t_0 \) is the time elapsed in the reference frame of the observer (in this case, the observer)
- \( v \) is the speed of the object
- \( v_0 \) is the speed of the object

In this case, \( t_0 = 3 \) years, \( v = 0.6c \), and \( v_0 = c \).

Let's calculate the time elapsed on Earth:

\[
t = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{1 - 0.36}} = \frac{3}{\sqrt{0.6}} \approx 3.33 \text{ years}
\]

Since the clock in the cockpit shows 3 years when the ship returns to Earth, the time on the Earth's clock is approximately 3.33 hours.

Since the two clocks are on the same ship, they will be identical due to the translational motion due to the ship's speed. Therefore, the time on the clock in the cargo bay will also be 3 years when the ship returns to Earth.
