<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \langle u(x, y), v(x, y), 0 \rangle\) is given by:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V}(x, y) = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
u(x, y) & v(x, y) & 0
\end{vmatrix}
\]
Expanding the determinant, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V}(x, y) = \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot 0 - \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \cdot v(x, y) \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot 0 - \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \cdot u(x, y) \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot v(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot u(x, y) \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Since \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is a function of \(x\) and \(y\) and does not depend on \(z\), the partial derivatives with respect to \(z\) are zero. Thus, the expression simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V}(x, y) = \left( 0 - 0 \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( 0 - 0 \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Thus, the curl is:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V}(x, y) = \boxed{\left( \frac{\partial v}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}}
\]
